/** @page sshgettingstarted Getting Started

    @tableofcontents

    This page shows the fastest path to a running SSH or SFTP server using the
    Qore SSH module. Both examples use the high-level
    @ref sshserverconnectionsintro "SshServerConnections" helpers so you can
    focus on the backend logic rather than low-level libssh configuration.

    @section sshgettingstartedprereqs Prerequisites

    - <b>Qore 2.3+</b> with @c %modern enabled
    - <b>libssh</b> installed (the binary module links against it)
    - The @c ssh binary module loaded (@c %requires @c ssh)
    - At least one SSH host key file (e.g. generated with
      @c ssh-keygen @c -t @c ed25519 @c -f @c ssh_host_ed25519_key @c -N @c "")

    @section sshgettingstartedsftp Minimal SFTP Server

    The example below creates an in-memory virtual SFTP server that accepts
    unauthenticated connections and serves a single file. It uses
    @ref SshServerConnections::SftpServerConnection "SftpServerConnection"
    with a @ref SftpServerDataProvider::VirtualSftpFilesystem "VirtualSftpFilesystem"
    backend.

    @code{.py}
    %modern
    %requires ssh
    %requires SshServerConnections
    %requires SshServerAuthProvider
    %requires SftpServerDataProvider

    SftpServerDataProvider::VirtualSftpFilesystem vfs();
    vfs.addOutboundText("hello.txt", "hello world\n");

    SshServerConnections::SftpServerConnection conn({
        "name": "my-sftp",
        "url": "sftpserver://127.0.0.1:0",
        "opts": {
            "host_keys": ("ssh_host_ed25519_key",),
            "auth": <SshServerAuthProvider::VirtualSshAuthProviderOptions>{
                "methods": ("none",),
                "none": <SshServerAuthProvider::VirtualSshStaticAuthMethodOptions>{
                    "success_decision": <SshServerAuthProvider::VirtualSshAuthDecisionTemplate>{
                        "principal": "anonymous",
                        "sftp_policy": {"mode": "enabled"},
                    },
                },
            },
            "backend": vfs,
        },
    });

    Qore::Ssh::SshServer server = conn.get(True);
    int port = server.getStatus().listeners[0].bound_port;
    printf("SFTP server listening on port %d\n", port);

    # ... serve sessions here ...
    server.stop();
    @endcode

    Port @c 0 lets the OS pick a free port; read the actual port from
    @c getStatus() as shown above.

    @note Threading contract: a single accepted @ref Qore::Ssh::SshSession
    "SshSession" must be driven by one thread at a time.  The underlying libssh
    session object is not thread-safe, so concurrent @c waitAuthContext() /
    @c waitCommandSession() / @c waitSftpSession() calls on the same session are
    rejected with @c SSHSESSION-STATE-ERROR.  Use one driver thread per session
    (distinct sessions may be driven concurrently).  The
    @ref Qore::Ssh::SftpSession "SftpSession" and
    @ref Qore::Ssh::SshCommandSession "SshCommandSession" objects of a session
    share its libssh session and are driven by the same rule; they remain valid
    after the session is disconnected or destroyed, and then report the
    disconnection with @c SFTPSESSION-STATE-ERROR or
    @c SSHCOMMANDSESSION-STATE-ERROR.  @c SshSession::disconnect() and
    @c SftpSession::close() may be called from another thread: they end a wait of
    the session (or of the SFTP session) at once, and the waiting thread raises
    @c SFTPSESSION-STATE-ERROR for an SFTP wait.

    @note Cancellation: the waits of all blocking operations (accept, key exchange,
    auth/command/SFTP waits, SFTP protocol reads) end as soon as the thread is
    cancelled with @c cancel_thread() or its @c Program is interrupted
    (@c SandboxManager), and raise @c THREAD-CANCELLED or @c PROGRAM-INTERRUPTED;
    this includes an indefinite-timeout SFTP read (the default @c timeout_ms of
    @c 0).  @c SshServer::stop() also ends the waits for a session at once.  With a
    %Qore library older than 3.0, the waits are cancelled within ~500ms instead.

    @section sshgettingstartedcommand Minimal SSH Command Server

    The example below creates a virtual command server that responds to
    @c exec requests with canned text output. It uses
    @ref SshServerConnections::SshCommandServerConnection "SshCommandServerConnection"
    with a @ref SshServerCommandProvider::VirtualSshCommandService "VirtualSshCommandService"
    command backend.

    @code{.py}
    %modern
    %requires ssh
    %requires SshServerConnections
    %requires SshServerAuthProvider
    %requires SshServerCommandProvider

    SshServerCommandProvider::VirtualSshCommandService service();
    service.addTextCommand("status", "ok\n");

    SshServerConnections::SshCommandServerConnection conn({
        "name": "my-command",
        "url": "sshserver://127.0.0.1:0",
        "opts": {
            "host_keys": ("ssh_host_ed25519_key",),
            "auth": <SshServerAuthProvider::VirtualSshAuthProviderOptions>{
                "methods": ("none",),
                "none": <SshServerAuthProvider::VirtualSshStaticAuthMethodOptions>{
                    "success_decision": <SshServerAuthProvider::VirtualSshAuthDecisionTemplate>{
                        "principal": "anonymous",
                        "command_policy": {"mode": "command-only"},
                    },
                },
            },
            "command_backend": service,
        },
    });

    Qore::Ssh::SshServer server = conn.get(True);
    int port = server.getStatus().listeners[0].bound_port;
    printf("SSH command server listening on port %d\n", port);

    # ... serve sessions here ...
    server.stop();
    @endcode

